  "answer_text": "Great question! Here is a comprehensive breakdown of where opioids come from:\n\n---\n\n## What Are Opioids Derived From?\n\n### \ud83c\udf38 The Opium Poppy \u2014 The Original Source\nOpioids are a class of drugs derived from substances that occur naturally in the **opium poppy** (*Papaver somniferum*) and are widely used for pain relief and sedation. The opium poppy is an herbaceous plant native to Turkey but is now present in all temperate climate areas.\n\nCultivation of the opium poppy for its active constituents is thought to have begun about 3000 BCE, and opioids that occur naturally in the plant have been used specifically for relieving pain and for producing euphoria since at least the time of the ancient Greeks.\n\n---\n\n### There are three main categories of opioids:\n\n### 1. \ud83c\udf3f Natural Opioids\nNatural opioids are substances derived directly from the opium poppy, typically from the **resin of the plant's seed pod**. Natural opioids are also referred to as **opiates**.\n\nThe natural alkaloids include **morphine** and **codeine**. Natural opioids such as codeine, heroin, morphine, and opium can be extracted from the opium poppy.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83e\uddea Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nSemi-synthetic opioids are made by **chemically processing natural opioids**, modifying them into new opioid compounds. While these drugs are man-made and don't occur in nature, natural opioids from the opium poppy serve as the \"raw material\" that pharmaceutical manufacturers (or drug traffickers) use to make them.\n\nExamples include:\n- **Hydrocodone**, prepared in 1920 by deriving it from codeine.\n- **Hydromorphone**, synthesized in 1924 by adding hydrogen to morphine.\n- **Heroin**, developed in 1898 by the German company Bayer from morphine.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83d\udd2c Fully Synthetic Opioids\nSynthetic opioids are **entirely man-made**. Unlike semi-synthetic opioids that are derived from compounds extracted from the opium poppy, synthetic opioids are made from **other precursor chemicals** that do not depend on the opium poppy at all.\n\nSynthetic opioid compounds are made to **mimic natural opioids** (as well as endorphins), allowing them to activate opioid receptors.\n\nThe first fully synthetic opioid was **meperidine (Demerol)**, found serendipitously by German chemist Otto Eisleb in 1932. It was the first opioid to have a structure unrelated to morphine but with opioid-like properties. Synthetic opioids include **fentanyl**, its analogues, and emerging non-fentanyl opioids.\n\n---\n\n### How Do They Work?\nOpioids such as heroin and morphine exert their effects by **mimicking naturally occurring substances** called endogenous opioid peptides, or **endorphins**. Opioids depress the central nervous system, meaning they slow down messages between the brain and the rest of the body.\n\n---\n\nIn summary, opioids originate from the **opium poppy plant**, but through chemistry and laboratory synthesis, many modern opioids are either partially or fully man-made, all designed to interact with the body's natural opioid receptors.",
